WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:03.660
- [David] Hello readers.

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.959
I would like to show you
one of my favorite things

00:00:05.959 --> 00:00:07.273
I ever wrote.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.720
It's this splash page

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.510
from this comic I wrote some years ago,

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.010
illustrated by my friend Cour Billadeau.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.200
You'll notice it has
almost no words in it,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.050
at least in this form.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.300
Now, let me show you the script.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.990
Pages three and four, panel one.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.800
A double-page spread,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.530
the whole greenhouse
revealed, in widescreen.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.740
It's glorious.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.410
Wisps of steam curl up from
bubbling patches of pool,

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.090
green kelpy tendrils twine
up the sides of the building.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.100
Everything is suffused with
a delicate, green glow.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.830
Absent the gro-lights,
some of the algae fluoresce

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.970
in a range of cool-spectrum colors.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.980
But primarily greens.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.050
Eugene is dwarfed by its
almost cosmic magnificence.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.150
Caption, I can't describe it.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.230
None of that language,
except for the caption,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.350
shows up in the final layout.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.100
All of that was me, the
writer, giving art direction

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.550
to Cour, the illustrator.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.321
Then they translated that
language into their own version.

00:01:05.321 --> 00:01:07.930
I think it's a pretty
faithful translation.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.450
My words are translated through
Cour's design sensibilities

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.370
and turned into artwork.

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.650
When you translate a written
work into a different medium,

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.240
whether it's illustrated,
animated, recorded,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.900
or performed live it changes.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.373
It becomes a fundamentally
different object.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.720
As readers, it's a
valuable skill to explore

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.710
how a text changes as it shifts
from one medium to another.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.640
And when I say, medium,
I mean a style of work,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.600
like a stage play, a
film, a graphic novel,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.840
and audio drama, any of that.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.490
Good readers analyze those changes

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.190
and use them to understand
decisions that creators make.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:48.940
If you're watching Romeo and Juliet

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.630
and the director has made the decision

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.160
to dress everyone in towering robot suits,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.660
a detail that is definitely
not in the play as written,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.670
what does that decision tell you?

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:58.910
What does it mean to you?

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.690
Maybe you have had the experience
of reading a book series

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:02.830
that was important to you

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:04.710
and then they made it into a movie

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.520
and you were so excited to see the movie,

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.700
and you sat down to see
it and it was awful.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.420
Like, they cast the wrong actors

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.890
and they simplified the
plot until it made no sense,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.320
and it just didn't feel right.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.010
That feeling is an
example of this tension,

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.440
this change, between a written material

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.690
and its visual adaptation.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.820
Sometimes it can be bad,
sometimes it can be good.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.492
Some readers can imagine
what characters in books

00:02:30.492 --> 00:02:32.260
look or sound like,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.260
hearing them in their mind's ear,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.670
seeing them in their mind's eye.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.260
The way your imagination
renders a character

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.410
is going to be different from

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.170
how someone else's imagination does it.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.690
So let's compare the
script for this audio drama

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.350
we've got on Khan Academy,
Hands Off My Phone,

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.860
and let's compare that
against the recorded version.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.950
So first, I'm going to put up the script

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.440
and I'd like you to pause the video

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.713
and just read it to yourself very quickly.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.840
And now let's take a listen
to the recorded version.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.670
(upbeat music)
(bell ringing)

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:12.040
- [Sebastian] Oh no he didn't.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.880
That is so harsh.
- [Valentina] Right?

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.130
- [Sebastian] Your dad
took your phone away,

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.940
just for getting a D on the history test?

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:17.773
- [Valentina] Yep.

00:03:17.773 --> 00:03:19.200
- [Sebastian] My mind is blown.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.260
- [Valentina] Mine too.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.930
- [Sebastian] I mean,
that was an easy test.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:22.763
- [Valentina] What?

00:03:22.763 --> 00:03:23.770
- [Sebastian] I can't believe you got a D.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:24.603
- [Valentina] Hey!

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:25.436
That's not really--

00:03:25.436 --> 00:03:26.650
- [Sebastian] And your dad
is the school principal,

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.460
and he took your phone away.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:29.293
Whoa.
(chuckling)

00:03:29.293 --> 00:03:32.060
- [Valentina] You know,
this isn't really helping.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.360
- [Sebastian] Your life
is so bleak right now.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.980
- [David] So you can read that script

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.650
and think, oh hey, Sebastian
is being earnestly sympathetic.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.800
Like, he really recognizes
that his friend is hurting,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:44.900
but when you hear that performance

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.110
the way I interpreted this line

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.210
is that Sebastian is
low-key roasting Valentina.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.570
He's not saying your life
is so bleak in a kind way.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.330
He's rubbing salt into the wounds

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.380
and being a dingus about it.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.010
There's also other stuff that's
not explicitly in the script

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.372
like, that opening musical sting.

00:04:02.372 --> 00:04:04.171
(upbeat music)

00:04:04.171 --> 00:04:07.120
And the ringing of the bell.
(bell ringing)

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.730
Or those background school noises.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.520
All of that is meant to represent the fact

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.860
that you're listening to a fictional show

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.773
that takes place in a school setting.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:17.910
Does it work?

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.383
That's for you to decide.

00:04:19.383 --> 00:04:22.250
When you watch or listen to a performance

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:24.910
or a recording, or when
you look at illustrations,

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.010
ask yourself some questions.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.440
For an audio piece, how
do the sound effects,

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.364
the acting decisions, and
the music impact the story

00:04:32.364 --> 00:04:36.530
or affect the meaning of the
words the characters say?

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.920
How does the way an actor delivers a line

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.530
change its emphasis?

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.090
Was someone being sarcastic or sincere

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.143
in a way you didn't expect, for example?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.500
For an illustrated story,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.830
how do those illustrations
help you understand

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.540
the author's tone and do you
agree with those choices?

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:55.244
Is the author's
interpretation of the story

00:04:55.244 --> 00:04:56.893
the same as yours?

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.100
For a TV show, or a movie, or a play,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.790
in addition to all the
decisions made by actors

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:06.240
about how to use their
voices, shape their faces,

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.500
or hold their bodies,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.880
how does the set tell the story?

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.340
How do costumes tell the story?

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.120
How does lighting or special
effects impact the tone?

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.640
For TV and film,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:21.180
how does the camera impact
the way the story is told?

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.380
The way a piece of film text is edited,

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.520
its cuts, its angles, its rhythms,

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.930
can help shape the way you, as a viewer,

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.381
as a reader of visual text, understand it.

00:05:32.381 --> 00:05:36.220
And if you can learn that,
then you can learn anything.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.053
David out.

